<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Multilingual Self – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Multilingual Self</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Benefits of Multilingualism</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Comprehend complex narrative texts. Reading: Analyze articles on societal trends.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend the main events in a narrative about bilingual life (e.g., "The student said they use both languages to communicate at home and school and explained how it helps them understand different perspectives.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can analyze an article on multilingualism trends, identifying the author's key arguments (e.g., "The article argues that being bilingual improves job prospects and helps you connect with more people, especially in Panama's diverse communities.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can summarize an audio segment on multilingual education in Panama, highlighting challenges and solutions (e.g., "The speaker said that students in Bocas del Toro benefit from practicing English with tourists, even though access to formal language courses can be limited.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 25px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map and points out countries known for multilingualism (e.g., Switzerland, India, Panama). Students are asked to *read* short quotes or facts projected on the board about famous multilingual individuals or benefits of multilingualism. They briefly *read* and discuss in pairs, "What language skills do these people possess? What opportunities do you believe multilingualism offers today, especially in business and education?" (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the previous lesson related to multilingualism, specifically focusing on word stress in "multilingualism," "multicultural," and "language learning." The teacher also models reading aloud one of the projected quotes with clear articulation, emphasizing the /s/ sound in "success" and /z/ sound in "citizens." (3 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the warm-up discussion and introduced vocabulary.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone has 'fluency' in a language, what does that mean they can do?" (Expected answer: "Communicate effectively and naturally.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'multilingualism' just about speaking two languages, or more?" (Expected answer: "More than two.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reinforces by asking students to give an example of a situation where "communication" is key. (2 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Multilingual Self".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher distributes an informational article (approx. 300-350 words) titled "Navigating My Multilingual World: A Panamanian Perspective on the Future of Language Skills." The article is a narrative/analytical piece discussing personal experiences with bilingual life and societal trends, incorporating the target vocabulary (multilingualism, proficiency, communication, career, opportunities, business, education, fluency, skill) and grammar (Reported Speech, Future Simple, Modals of Ability). The text includes quotes from hypothetical Panamanian youth discussing their experiences, making it a complex narrative text and an analysis of societal trends.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student comprehends main ideas and significant details in extended texts on familiar and abstract topics within their field of interest, inferring meaning from context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the article silently for gist. (3 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Reading (Gist):</b> Students answer the question: "What is the main idea the author wants to communicate about language learning in Panama?" (Students write their answer on a sticky note and place it on a designated area.) (3 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Reading (True/False & Detail):</b> Students re-read the article and answer 4-5 True/False statements related to specific details and arguments presented in the text. Example T/F: "The article argues that speaking multiple languages will not significantly impact future job opportunities." (Expected answer: False). They also match short summary sentences to relevant paragraphs. (5 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension for specific information, identifying author's arguments, analyzing use of Reported Speech, Future Simple, and Modals of Ability. Target vocabulary: multilingualism, proficiency, communication, career, opportunities, business, education, fluency, skill; said, argued, believe, prioritize, offer, communicate, become, excel, enhance, learn; bilingual, multilingual, proficient, effective, international, diverse, multicultural, necessary, fluent.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Vocabulary and Grammar Spotting (5 min):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences directly from the article, but with key vocabulary words (e.g., *proficiency, career, business*) or grammatical structures (e.g., reported speech verbs like 'said,' 'argued'; future simple 'will prioritize') missing. Students refer to the article to fill in the blanks, ensuring correct word usage and grammatical form. For instance, a sentence might be "One student ______ that multilingualism opens new ______ for a successful ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Argument Identification (5 min):</b> In pairs, students re-read paragraphs 2 and 3 of the article. They are instructed to identify and underline two key arguments the author *argues* in favor of multilingualism. They then rewrite these arguments using reported speech. Example: "The author argued that multilingual individuals can enhance their chances in the international job market." They also identify one prediction made using the future simple (e.g., "Companies *will prioritize* hiring employees with strong language *skills*.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Inferring Meaning and Modals (5 min):</b> Students work with their partner to answer inferential questions about the article. For example: "Based on the article, what *can* students in Panama do to improve their language *proficiency*?" or "What *opportunities* do you believe students *can* find in diverse communities like Panama City or Bocas del Toro by using their language *skills*?" They must use modals of ability (can, could, might) in their answers and discuss the pragmatics of connecting with diverse cultures.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Multilingual Self".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: Collaborative Article Analysis and Discussion Points</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group is given a short, supplementary text (another paragraph or two) that either expands on or offers a slightly different perspective on the benefits of multilingualism in Panama or globally. Students collectively *read* and *analyze* this new text. Their task is to identify the main arguments presented in the new text and compare them to the original article.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Groups must prepare 3-4 discussion points or questions for the class, synthesizing information from both texts. They should formulate their points using reported speech (e.g., "The new article suggested that..."), future simple for predictions (e.g., "We believe this trend *will become* even more prominent..."), and modals of ability (e.g., "Panamanian citizens *can enhance* their global *opportunities* if they develop high language *proficiency*."). This activity promotes discussing language diversity and promoting cultural inclusiveness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Finally, each group briefly presents their discussion points to the class, encouraging a whole-class conversation on the topic. (10 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group work and a short individual written response to assess reading comprehension and analysis skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students write a short paragraph (5-7 sentences) summarizing the main article "Navigating My Multilingual World: A Panamanian Perspective on the Future of Language Skills." They must identify at least two key arguments the author presents regarding the benefits of multilingualism and include at least one example of reported speech and one prediction using the future simple, derived from their reading.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate identification and summary of the author's key arguments (Reading: Analyze articles on societal trends).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (multilingualism, career, opportunities, proficiency, skill).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of reported speech and future simple.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the written summary.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I learned about multilingualism from today's reading is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/challenging to (circle one) identify the author's main arguments because..."</li>
                </ul>
                (7 min)
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did analyzing the article help me understand the benefits of multilingualism in my own context or for my future career?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategy (skimming, scanning, inferring) did I use most effectively today, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The reading tasks successfully engaged students in identifying key arguments and specific details. The integration of reported speech and future simple from the text was effective for grammar practice. Some students needed more scaffolding to connect the article's broader societal trends to their personal scenario ("My Multilingual Self"). Next time, I will provide more explicit examples of how *they* can apply the advice from the article to their own language learning backgrounds. The focus on /s/ and /z/ pronunciation was good for word awareness, but could be reinforced more actively in spoken practice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on *reading* about the benefits of multilingualism. In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will *actively speak and interact* using the arguments and vocabulary from this article to discuss their personal experiences and opinions about multilingualism, further promoting cultural inclusiveness and offering practical, culturally sensitive advice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>